<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">poem May <a href="/years/1918">1918</a>; music <a href="/years/1921">1921</a>; co-national anthem from <a href="/years/1938">1938</a>, sole anthem <a href="/years/1957">1957</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">In May 1918 C. J. Langenhoven wrote an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> poem called &quot;Die Stem&quot; (&quot;The Voice&quot;). The Reverend Marthinus Lourens de Villiers set it to music in 1921; the tune that was accepted was his second, since the first did not satisfy Langenhoven. In the 1920s the South African Broadcasting Corporation played it at the close of each day's broadcasts, together with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The anthem was first recorded in 1926, when Betty Steyn performed its first and third verses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for the Zonophone record label. It was sung in public for the first time on 31 May 1928, at the raising of the new South African national flag. In 1938 the government proclaimed it one of two co-national anthems, together with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot;.</p><p>From 1952 it was sung in English as well as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, and both versions had official status. &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the Queen</a>&quot; ceased to be a co-national anthem in May 1957, though it remained the royal anthem until 1961, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> stopped being a Commonwealth realm. The poem originally had three verses; the government asked Langenhoven to add a fourth with a religious theme.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The song is uplifting in tone and speaks throughout of commitment to the Vaderland (Fatherland) and to God. One verse expresses dedication to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> and another recalls the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-trek">Great Trek</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voortrekkers">Voortrekkers</a>. The English version is for the most part a faithful translation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a>, with a few minor changes; it leaves out the specific mention of Afrikaners, to avoid alienating British-descent whites who were not counted as Afrikaners.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Many black South Africans disliked the anthem, seeing it as triumphalist and closely tied to the apartheid regime. State President P. W. Botha, by contrast, was fond of it. When South African teams returned to international sport in the early 1990s, agreements were made with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> that it would not be sung at rugby matches. At a test against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> in 1992 the crowd nevertheless sang it, and an instrumental recording was played over the loudspeakers, which caused controversy. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/1992-summer-olympics">1992 Summer Olympics</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> the South African team used Beethoven's setting of Schiller's &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ode-to-joy">Ode to Joy</a>&quot; instead.</p><p>A commission seeking a new anthem in 1993 received 119 entries and chose none. The song kept its status after the 1994 general election and shared it with &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a>&quot; until 1997, when a composite anthem joined parts of both. Its place in the later anthem has been disputed: some South Africans have called for those parts to be removed because of the apartheid connection, while others defend them as a reconciling gesture. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> version remained popular with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalist groups.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Die Stem van Suid-Afrika” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1994, after the end of apartheid, President Nelson Mandela declared both &quot;Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika&quot; and the former anthem, &quot;<b>Die Stem van Suid-Afrika</b>&quot;, national anthems of South Africa, and the two were played together.</blockquote><a href="/songs/nkosi-sikelel-iafrika">Nkosi Sikelel' iAfrika</a></li></ul></section>
